    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0225" n="149" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">660F.116/171: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Czechoslovakia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wright</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Praha</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-21T15:00:00+01:00">June 21, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 21—12:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. Your No. 17, June 18, 3 p.m., and instruction No. 49. Increased duty on all
                    lard imports, effective January 1st last as reported by Commercial Attaché in
                    his trade notes No. 464 of December 28, 1934,<anchor n="20" xml:id="d118-f1"/>
                    more intensive competition from local product and artificial substitutes,
                    together with higher cost of American lard make it practically impossible for
                    the latter now to find a market in this country irrespective of quota. Leading
                    importer of American lard is selling off his stock in hand at considerable loss
                    and will make no further imports for the present. Am pressing for equitable
                    solution. Full details by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wright</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
